.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_TOTAL_CAPACITY 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_total_capacity \- report the number of elements a pool holds
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_total_capacity(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the total number of pool-owned elements that mp holds, counting
both the elements in use and the free ones. It does not count a dynamic
fallback entry; see ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) for those.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of elements that the pool holds
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This is the elem_count that you gave to ccol_mempool_create(), or, for a
pool from ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(), the element
count that the library derived from the buffer. It does not change over
the lifetime of the pool.

A thread-safe pool large enough to keep a cache for each thread
allocates extra elements above this count as a reserve, which keeps the
advertised count available to any thread no matter how many entries
other threads hold in their caches. This call does not report the
reserve, and the reserve does not change the memory use of the pool at
run time: ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3) reports the whole block, the
reserve included, and the library allocates that block once, when it
creates the pool.

The two ends of the count are not equally exact for such a pool. A
thread takes an entry from its own cache with no lock held, and so with
no count to consult, which means entries go into a cache before anybody
knows whether they are needed. That decision favours the guarantee
above: the pool never refuses a caller while the cache of another thread
holds entries that the first caller was entitled to. In exchange,
concurrent callers can hold a few entries more than this count at one
time, for a short time, but never more than the physical slot count. A
pool with no cache is exact in both directions; every pool created with
single_threaded = true is such a pool, and so is every pool that
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(3) built. There an
allocation past this count fails, or falls back to the system allocator.

See ccol_mempool_create(3) for how the pool size affects performance.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of the pool, which makes it a cold-path call:
do not use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
